WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
Rotifers graze on algae in a chemostat.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.500
That is a vessel with a steady flow of nutrient medium in, and a matching flow out.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.400
Both populations rise and fall in cycles.

4
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.880
Watch when the rotifers peak.

5
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.700
They peak after the algae do, but how long after?

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:29.820
That delay is a clue, and this film asks what it can tell us.

7
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:36.603
In classical predator-prey models, the predator peak lags the prey peak by about a quarter of a cycle.

8
00:00:38.523 --> 00:00:43.983
Prey rise, predators feed and multiply, prey crash, predators starve.

9
00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:50.023
The phase lag is the delay between peaks, as a fraction of the cycle's period.

10
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:55.823
Phase lag in cycles equals delay in days over period in days.

11
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.627
Here is an invented example.

12
00:00:59.487 --> 00:01:01.107
Assume the cycles are regular.

13
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:07.327
Prey peak on days 10, 40 and 70, so the period is 30 days.

14
00:01:08.327 --> 00:01:15.147
In run A, the rotifers peak on day 17.5. That is 7.5 days after the prey.

15
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:20.387
7.5 over 30 is a quarter cycle, the classical lag.

16
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:25.987
In run B, the rotifers peak on day 25 instead.

17
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:29.207
That is 15 days after the prey.

18
00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:33.127
15 over 30 is one half cycle.

19
00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:37.427
The predator peaks now sit at the prey troughs.

20
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:39.867
That pattern is called antiphase.

21
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:46.350
Yoshida and colleagues grew the rotifer Brachionus on the alga Chlorella.

22
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.850
The alga was either one clone or several.

23
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:54.550
One clone cannot evolve by selection among clones.

24
00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:01.710
Several can. Single-clone prey gave short cycles with the classical quarter lag.

25
00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:07.310
Multiclonal prey gave long cycles, with peaks largely out of step.

26
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.090
In the real experiment the two treatments did not share a period.

27
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.130
In our example, run B resembles the evolving-prey runs.

28
00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:22.303
But the lag alone does not prove that the algae evolved.

29
00:02:22.863 --> 00:02:25.063
A long lag could have other causes.

30
00:02:25.923 --> 00:02:31.563
The causal case came from changing the clonal diversity, and from a measured trade-off.

31
00:02:31.563 --> 00:02:36.643
Algae that resisted grazing were poorer competitors for nutrients.

32
00:02:37.503 --> 00:02:39.523
The study showed prey evolution.

33
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:42.263
It did not investigate predator evolution.

34
00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:43.963
So keep this rule.

35
00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:46.263
A phase lag is a pattern.

36
00:02:46.263 --> 00:02:50.343
The causal case comes from changing the prey's variation.

